            <ArticleTitle>The Dynamics of the Gut Microbiome in Multiple Sclerosis in Relation to Disease.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>185-196</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0733-8619(17)30095-6</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.ncl.2017.08.008</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Multiple sclerosis (MS) is a neuroinflammatory autoimmune disease of unknown etiology, although genetic components and environmental triggers are thought to collude to commence pathogenesis. Numerous investigations are now demonstrating the role of the gut microbiota in neuroinflammation and how alterations in its content may be associated with MS disease. This article explores the studies using MS rodent models to determine the roles of gut bacteria in neuroinflammatory disease, evaluate the evidence linking gut bacterial dysbiosis and MS, and give insight into potential MS therapies targeting the gut microbiota currently under investigation.</AbstractText>
